  "mechanismSummary": "Piperidine removes the acidic proton at the five-membered ring carbon of the fluorenyl group; the anion is aromatic. It expels the carbamate in an E1cB elimination, giving dibenzofulvene. A second piperidine adds to the dibenzofulvene (a conjugate addition) and the carbanion is protonated. The carbamate takes a proton on nitrogen and loses CO₂, freeing the amine. The trapping and the loss of CO₂ happen in no fixed order; the app follows one. Fmoc is removed by base and survives acid, the opposite of Boc: in solid-phase peptide synthesis, Fmoc protects the α-amino group and acid-labile tert-butyl groups protect the side chains.",
  "references": [
    "Clayden, Greeves and Warren, Organic Chemistry, 2nd edition (Oxford University Press), chapter 23",
    "Wuts, Greene's Protective Groups in Organic Synthesis, 5th edition (Wiley), chapter 7 (carbamates)"
  ],
